---
title: Network Interfaces
order: 1
description: ip link, ip addr, ip route, ip neigh, bridge
tags: [ip, interface, route, arp, bridge, link, addr]
---

The iproute2 suite replaces legacy ifconfig/route/arp tools. All subcommands
accept abbreviated forms (e.g. `ip a` for `ip addr`).

@code bash
# ---- ip link: manage network interfaces ----

ip link show                        # list all interfaces
ip link show dev eth0               # show specific interface
ip link set eth0 up                 # bring interface up
ip link set eth0 down               # bring interface down
ip link set eth0 mtu 9000           # set MTU
ip link set eth0 promisc on         # enable promiscuous mode
ip link set eth0 name lan0          # rename interface (must be down)
ip link set eth0 master br0         # attach to bridge
ip link set eth0 nomaster           # detach from bridge

# virtual interfaces
ip link add veth0 type veth peer name veth1   # veth pair
ip link add bond0 type bond                    # bonding
ip link add dummy0 type dummy                  # dummy interface
ip link delete veth0                           # remove virtual interface

@text
Interface naming: Artix/Arch uses predictable names (enp3s0, wlp2s0) by
default. To see the mapping between driver and predictable names, check
`/sys/class/net/*/device/uevent`.

@code bash
# ---- ip addr: manage IP addresses ----

ip addr show                              # all addresses
ip addr show dev eth0                     # specific interface
ip addr show scope global                 # only globally-routed addresses
ip -4 addr show                           # IPv4 only
ip -6 addr show                           # IPv6 only

ip addr add 192.168.1.10/24 dev eth0                  # add address
ip addr add 192.168.1.10/24 brd + dev eth0             # add with auto broadcast
ip addr add 192.168.1.10/24 dev eth0 label eth0:1      # add alias
ip addr del 192.168.1.10/24 dev eth0                   # remove address
ip addr flush dev eth0                                  # remove all addresses

@code bash
# ---- ip route: manage routing table ----

ip route show                             # show main routing table
ip route show table all                   # show all tables
ip route get 8.8.8.8                      # query route for destination

ip route add default via 192.168.1.1 dev eth0              # default gateway
ip route add 10.0.0.0/8 via 192.168.1.1                   # static route
ip route add 10.0.0.0/8 via 192.168.1.1 metric 100        # with metric
ip route del 10.0.0.0/8                                    # remove route
ip route replace default via 192.168.1.254                 # replace existing
ip route flush table main                                  # flush all routes

# policy routing
ip rule show                                              # list rules
ip rule add from 192.168.1.0/24 table 100                 # source-based routing
ip route add default via 10.0.0.1 table 100

@code bash
# ---- ip neigh: ARP / neighbor table ----

ip neigh show                             # show ARP cache
ip neigh show dev eth0                    # specific interface
ip neigh add 192.168.1.5 lladdr aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff dev eth0   # static entry
ip neigh del 192.168.1.5 dev eth0        # remove entry
ip neigh flush dev eth0                  # flush entries

@code bash
# ---- bridge: manage bridge interfaces ----

ip link add br0 type bridge               # create bridge
ip link set br0 up
ip link set eth0 master br0               # add port to bridge
ip link set eth1 master br0
bridge link show                          # show bridge ports
bridge fdb show                           # forwarding database
bridge vlan show                          # VLAN info

# alternative: brctl (bridge-utils, legacy)
brctl addbr br0                           # create
brctl addif br0 eth0                      # add interface
brctl show                                # list bridges
brctl showmacs br0                        # MAC table

@text
Persistence: iproute2 commands are not persistent across reboots. For
persistence, use netifrc scripts in /etc/conf.d/net.* (OpenRC) or create
a custom init script. NetworkManager and ConnMan also handle persistence.

@code bash
# ---- useful flags ----

ip -s link show                   # statistics (rx/tx bytes, errors)
ip -d link show                   # detailed info (driver, etc.)
ip -j addr show                   # JSON output
ip -j -p route show               # JSON pretty-printed
ip -c addr show                   # colorized output
ip monitor                        # monitor netlink events in real time
